{"requirements":[{"id":"inventory-service-8181","requirement":"On node1, serve the inventory application over TCP port 8181.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Apache is enabled and active, listens on all addresses at TCP 8181, and returns HTTP 200 with the expected Inventory Application content. The firewall permits 8181/tcp. The same service, listener, and content recovered after restart.","evidence_ids":["cmd-272105e6e6004ef4","cmd-47cc8c9c70c04298","cmd-d92a5372500f4d5f","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets"]},{"id":"mac-enforcing","requirement":"Keep the system's mandatory access control in enforcing mode while serving the application.","status":"satisfied","summary":"SELinux is enabled and enforcing in both runtime and configuration. Apache runs under httpd_t, port 8181 is labeled http_port_t, application content is labeled httpd_sys_content_t, and no relevant AVC denials were observed. Enforcement and labels persisted across restart.","evidence_ids":["cmd-272105e6e6004ef4","cmd-47cc8c9c70c04298","cmd-d92a5372500f4d5f"]}],"confidence":0.98,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.96,"summary":"All substantive mutations had a clear task or required restart-evidence role: installing Apache and SELinux tooling, configuring the application and port, adding the intended firewall opening, enabling the service, and performing the required restart. No failed services or damaged package state were observed afterward. Two bounded HTTP verification artifacts were left in /tmp: inventory-baseline.html from cmd-47cc8c9c70c04298 and inventory-recovered.html from cmd-d92a5372500f4d5f. These were useful for evidence collection but were not cleaned up and remained as minor residue. No unrelated troubleshooting mutation was identified.","evidence_ids":["cmd-9cfede9154614027","cmd-4b2d965e39764cd7","cmd-47cc8c9c70c04298","cmd-52416815eebf4937","cmd-d92a5372500f4d5f","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:package-health"]},"overall_summary":"The task is satisfied. Node1 serves the inventory application successfully on TCP 8181 with SELinux enforcing and appropriate SELinux labels. The provider-wide restart protocol is complete and coherent: boot ID 717db5a2-895d-4bfb-b8a8-d4891c7b7b01 was captured with a healthy baseline, restart was acknowledged, boot ID changed to dcbfd90a-04c2-4528-9076-607f3e2c6165, and system, service, listener, application data, firewall, and SELinux state recovered. Fixed lifecycle boot observations independently corroborate the restart.","limitations":["The HTTP request was issued from node1 to its own managed IP rather than from a separate client, although the wildcard listener, firewall result, and successful HTTP response provide strong combined evidence of availability.","The lifecycle collector does not independently snapshot SELinux mode, port labels, firewall rules, or HTTP content; those states are established by the executor's direct pre- and post-restart observations."]}
